WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.700
Film Courage: Everyone can write but is everyone a
writer? Writer/Instructor and Script Consultant Shannan E. Johnson (The Professional Pen): Great question. Everyone can write

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.620
and everyone can be a writer
but I think the bigger question is what

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.099
part of the creative process are you
trying to be a part of and a lot of

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:17.010
people don't know that there are so many
other ways of being a part of the

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.670
creative process in this particular
industry that you don't have to be a

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.900
writer so I think because of the lack of
knowledge people know there's a director

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.260
they know there's a writer they know
there's a producer some people might not

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.940
think past those three things right and
so they're thinking to themselves a lot

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.880
of people think well I want to be the
writer because the writer is the person

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.090
who's in charge you know they're the
ones who are making the story they're

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:43.500
the ones who get to say what happens
little do they know that the writer is

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.000
not in charge the writer puts that stuff
on the script they may receive their

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.300
paycheck and they may not be a part of
the rest of the process because there

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.980
are other people who have creative jobs
throughout the process so I definitely

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.030
think anybody can be a writer but I do
think that they have to want to hone the

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:04.379
skill and they have to respect the art
but in but other than that some people

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.260
and this is what I enjoyed about going
to Florida State's film school because

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.439
we did get to learn all of the
below-the-line positions some people get

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:13.650
in and realize I don't want to be the
director I want to be the camera

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.030
operator everyone thinks that the camera
operator is the director right like if

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.330
you know people who are at home who
might just within a movie see a movie

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.020
being made they assume that the person
who's actually behind the camera is the

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.810
director and it's like no the directors
over there in the chair watching the

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.560
monitor you know so they can see the
whole frame etc and some people really

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.520
want to be the person physically out
there with the camera so I think if we

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.900
just had more knowledge about all of the
parts of the process and how you can be

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.799
a part of that process many people
wouldn't choose writing because even if

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:47.490
we just think about it in general a lot
of people just aren't aren't that great

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.299
with the english/language period a lot
of people don't like to write period you

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:54.869
know some people don't like to read
that's just that their thing that's

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:58.979
totally fine but to then have this
creative thing that you want to get out

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:02.610
it's like yeah well you can be a non
writing producer you can be the person

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:07.290
who has the ideas and then hire someone
who can actually do the physical writing

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.500
because they've you know been able to
master that skill in some kind of way

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.420
but I think it just really comes from a
place of having idea

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.440
and not knowing how they can fit into
the overall puzzle of the industry and

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:22.209
have a creative voice so then going back
to that hypothetical person that wakes

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:25.060
up one day and says I want to be a
writer and a really cool story

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.650
everyone's gonna love this they need to
know that that's not exactly how it

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.920
works and it takes a lot of time and
what do you say to that one that you

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.180
said the person at home watching TV and
maybe they did have an incredible life

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:41.709
and they overcame a lot of challenges
but they're not really writers yeah I

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:46.150
think it's hard to make people
understand that they're not writers

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:52.269
right I think just like with the example
of thinking of an athlete people think

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:56.350
they know what athletes look like people
think they know what the skill sets

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.440
should be if you're an athlete some
people have athletic bodies you might

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.830
not know what kind of sport they play
right but they walk into the room and

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.430
you're thinking oh you're probably some
kind of athlete with writers there's no

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.970
additional thing like that because
anybody can be a writer

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:15.819
so I say if you have an idea respect the
craft enough to then go hone your skill

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:19.390
that doesn't mean you have to go to film
school film school is not for everyone

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.470
I enjoy film school because I like
learning in a classroom structure right

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:27.400
but I could have had that same education
by just going and being on some sets I

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.660
think all writers should PA at some time
all writers should be on set so that you

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.190
can see what it takes to get done what
you're asking people to do right so you

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.790
can understand what budgets actually
look like when you're writing your car

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.480
chasing and you know writing your action
scenes and understanding that needing

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.840
blood and eating prosthetics and needing
stunt doubles and eating all these

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.250
people all of that is gonna cost money
right so just hone your skill hone your

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:56.500
craft there are online courses I teach
online courses I give feedback on on

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.940
screenplays because I'm not saying don't
try you definitely should try but within

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.510
that process get some feedback and get
better one of the things that I tell

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.620
people is if this is actually your baby
if you think this this is the best idea

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:14.769
idea ever don't practice on your baby
practice on some other idea so that you

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:18.400
can get good and then write your baby
because you want people to take your

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.760
baby seriously the other thing is I tell
people stop calling it your baby it's

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.280
not your baby at some point you're gonna
have to sell your baby

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.710
and you don't want to write so realize
that it's art and it's there for other

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:34.280
people to participate in the process and
on your side of it if you're trying to

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.190
be a professional writer because not
everybody is trying to be a professional

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.889
writer some people just have an idea and
that's the thing they want to get made

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:44.660
some people actually want to have
writing as their profession meaning this

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:48.290
is how they make money this is how they
feed their families and those people are

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.590
the ones that really have to let go of
the baby because you just need to be

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.400
writing things so that you can be making
income right so your baby somebody

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.400
else's baby whoever's baby you'll write
whatever you need to write so that you

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.880
can have a consistent income but for
those people who just have an idea and

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:06.140
that's the thing that they want to get
made do the work make sure that it's

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.800
good stuff find some other people to
come in and be a part of the process I

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.160
tell writers all the time if you're not
a director and you don't want to be a

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.840
director go find someone to direct your
to direct your script because if you're

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.500
gonna take the time to put in the money
and the energy of actually making the

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.010
film then you want that product to look
good so what if you write a good

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:28.610
screenplay but you don't get the people
necessary so that you can make it look

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.850
good on the screen and then now the
thing that you have to represent your

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.910
screenplay
is not representing it very well right

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.229
the same thing is true if you're a
director but you're not a writer and you

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:43.250
don't want to learn how to be a writer
go find you one of your writer friends

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:47.630
who has a script and then you can direct
that there so this is a collaborative

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:52.340
process it's collaborative effort and as
writers we a lot of times get stuck with

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:56.090
thinking it's just me and my computer
right it's like well no there's so many

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:00.020
other people who participate in this
process so anybody who has an idea can

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:04.820
write it whether it will be good whether
it will fit the structure whether it's

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:11.180
something that is a part of the art you
know because I get stuck in that place

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.140
of like just respect the art you know go
out there and get the information that

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:19.610
you need so that you can have something
that's great on the page and when you

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:24.440
hear certain code words or you see a
demeanor in someone how do you know that

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:27.470
ooh this one these people where we're
gonna have to have a talk because

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:34.220
they're too precious with their material
let's see I think I think the code words

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:38.460
are as simple as they're always talking
about my

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.949
and well when I get there they'll know
and I'll tell them in blah blah blah and

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:48.090
I always have to say to them you might
not be there like you just physically

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:53.130
might not be there just understanding
the potentially in features they may

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:57.270
literally pay for your script and send
you on your way and never ask you

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:03.150
another thing right so just explaining
to them the writers place in the process

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.900
like I even have a Hollywood pyramid
that I show people that I made and I

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.699
show them who is king the studios are
king the networks are the team of the

00:07:11.699 --> 00:07:15.180
King production companies are not far
under them then you have all of the

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.930
executives you have all of the above the
line people and then the writer is way

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:24.120
down at the bottom because they are just
the first part of the process and after

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.630
they're part of the process is over
everybody else has to do theirs right

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.810
it's one of the reasons that no one
invites the writers not no one but

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:34.500
people don't often invite the writers to
the set because they don't want to hear

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.319
the writers going well this is what it
was supposed to be in blah blah blah now

00:07:37.319 --> 00:07:41.250
they're giving notes and it's like that
part of the process is the directors

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:45.630
part of the process he or she is now
bringing his or her vision to your

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:49.650
script so you don't know what direction
it's going in now and now you're invited

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:53.460
to set and you're on set giving notes to
actor who's in talking to all of these

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.889
people and you're now out of line you've
now stepped outside of your bounds so

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:02.190
it's really easy to see when people
don't know that that's how the process

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:08.430
goes so I always just say hey I just
want to give you what the what the help

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:11.849
you set your expectation of what this
will be and it doesn't mean that there

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:15.780
aren't exceptions there are exceptions
there are people who write their films

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.280
and they're also able to be producers
and be there along the way so it's

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:24.300
possible does it happen often for
someone who's writing their very first

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:29.340
film not at all you know and that's just
a part of the a part of the business and

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.159
so a lot of people don't have the
knowledge of the business and that's one

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.310
of the reasons why I exist as a script
consultant so that you can call me and

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:39.000
ask me and I can just tell you what it
is and again anytime I give people if I

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.050
saw I always tell them somebody did it
another way like this is not the kind of

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:48.000
industry that has a step-by-step this is
how you make it kind of thing not for

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.570
actors not for writers not for anybody
everybody gets there and

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:56.280
different way so it's totally possible
that you can get that get there the way

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.140
that you want to get there but generally
speaking this is how things work in the

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.620
industry

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.480
you

